eu and japanese leaders have agreed to  strengthen their partnership in the wake  of russia's invasion of ukraine european  commission president usually vandalian  accompanied by european council  president shal michel held talks with  japanese prime minister fumio kishida in  tokyo the sides also agreed on a new  digital partnership which will among  other things focus on improving the  global supply of crucial semiconductors  later addressing a joint press  conference the leader spoke of stepping  up sanctions against russia and greater  cooperation  russia's invasion of ukraine shakes the  foundations of the international order  not only in europe but also in asia  and this is absolutely unacceptable  in cooperation with the g7 including the  eu  japan will implement strong sanctions  against russia and strengthened support  to ukraine  let me finish prime minister  by thanking you warmly  for the admirable solidarity that japan  showed  by diverting some of its lng supplies to  europe  it was at a crucial time for us  at the height of the european heating  season  and we will not forget this  this shows the power and the solidarity  of democracies working together and  joining me now from tokyo for more on  this is michito suruoka he's an  associate professor at care university  and specializes in international  security and european politics professor  suroqua japan and the eu have always  been close partners but what impact has  two years of covet 19 and the ukraine  war had on this partnership  okay the ukraine war  has a lot of a big big impact on the eu  japan relationship which is the scene as  a sort of catalyst for more contact and  more closer coordination between the two  because what we are seeing now is an  unprecedented level of coordination and  dialogue between the two partners mainly  on ukraine economic sanctions against  ukraine but at the same time in the g7  context as well japan and the eul  the the part of g7 process and also in  the context of g7 we have seen a huge  number of foreign ministers meetings and  summit meetings  so the level of coordination and  cooperation between japan andy both  bilaterally and also in the context of  g7 has increased quite a lot  now prime minister kishida talked of the  ukraine war shaking the foundation of  the international order not only in  europe but also in asia  what does he mean by that  yes though what prime minister shira has  been emphasizing  is the fact that the european security  and the security in the pacific  are indivisible  so there's a huge linkage between the  two and also particularly the we we we  need to prevent  uh the the the change of status quo by  force  from taking place again in asia so that  that is why we should not uh  we should not allow the war in ukraine  as a precedent for another crisis or  another invasion in the indo-pacific  region so that's what  the tokyo is very much uh focusing on  right now in the context of the linkage  between two regions  so in the context of this is tokyo  essentially talking about beijing and  the china's assertiveness in the region  yeah that's what we are very much  focusing on because because of  geographical proximity and also the  rising  tensions in south china sea and east  china sea and uh so that's what the  tokyo needs to address  so the yeah so it's i think it's just  natural for japan to look at the linkage  between the two regions and the linkage  between  russia and china and relations between  the two countries  now speaking of linkages japan like  europe has a very close trade  relationship with china  but  far difficult bilateral relations as  compared to most countries in europe uh  i wonder therefore where does the eu fit  into japan's strategy  on that front  in dealing with china you mean  correct yes  the  yes in terms of dealing with china  the  china is a big neighbor and china is  really one of the most important trading  partners for japan so the  business community in japan  is very much interested in doing  continuing business with china of course  and that's also very much in japan's  national interest  and but at the same time we have various  a wide range of concerns about china  and  in terms of dealing with  various economic problems then the  japan needs to get other like-minded  countries on board so we very much  cooperate with the united states of  course but at the same time the eu is a  like-minded partner sharing values and  sharing various concerns about china as  well so that is why there is a growing  interest in japan in working more  closely with the eu  so the in terms of ensuring the market  access for example  or or the treatment of private companies  investors in china  and the the intellectual property rights  and the diseases as well  so the and also the we are increasingly  uh interested in cooperating with  americans and european partners in terms  of dealing with human rights issues and  hong kong issues as well  so the  in the context of the war in ukraine  those  china related issues are now  seen as more invisible but but the  challenges are there and uh perhaps the  challenges are increasing so the i think  the fact that eu leaders  visited japan  amid the war in ukraine i think it's a  great demonstration that the europeans  are  still very much engaged in the  indo-pacific region  and uh certainly not forgetting the  importance of this region so just that  that i think was a great  powerful message so this week actually  we also saw a a visit by finnish prime  minister right so the  so this is sort of a return of in-person  diplomacy  i i think it  is great after after two two years of  pandemic right  a pleasure talking to you thank you for  bringing that perspective to us  professor michito suruoka from ko  university thank you sir  you
 

